    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The first factor vanishes at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. The cosine factor vanishes when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> in the interval. Tangent is undefined at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, which is excluded.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> On the successive open intervals separated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, the product signs are respectively negative, positive, negative, positive, positive. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> two factors change sign together, so the product keeps its positive sign either side.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The nonpositive set is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> together with the isolated zero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. Its interval length is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, answer B.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The isolated zero is a solution but adds no length. The tangent pole cannot be included even though nearby intervals may satisfy the sign condition.</p>"
